{
 "_doc": "How each kind of tree grows in 3D (scripts/world3d/tree_model.gd). A tree's shape comes from its catalog family (apple, lemon...), or for scenery trees from its id; anything unlisted grows as 'round'. form picks how the crown is built: round (a dome of leafy clumps), oval (taller than wide), spreading (low and wide on angled limbs), dense (a tight glossy ball on a short trunk, the citrus look), weeping (strands hanging to the ground), palm (a tall ringed trunk and a crown of fronds), banana (a soft stem and huge paddle leaves), bushy (several stems from the ground). Sizes are shares of a full tree on one tile: trunk_h and trunk_r its trunk, crown_r and crown_h its crown, clumps the leafy clumps in it (palms and bananas), tall scales a form's height (each tree varies 0.8 to 1.3 of it too), masses the big leaf masses of other crowns (reach: how far out they sit as a share of crown_r, flat: how squashed, mass: their size). leaf is the leaf colour; shine makes it glossy. leaf_alts and fruit_alts list other leaf and fruit colours a tree of that family may have instead (each tree picks one, so a row of one kind is not one colour). blossom adds flowers through the crown in that colour. fruit_shape: round, oval, long (a hanging bunch), big (a few huge ones on the trunk), small (nuts, olives: many tiny ones), cluster (dates: hanging bunches). Every tree is also varied a little by its own seed: height, lean, the clumps' places and shades.",
